  "notes": "Multi-instrumentalist teen-pop-country — \"the most overtly pop-arranged male act of the early 2010s.\" The drums are programmed-first by the entry's own account: \"claps, 16th hats, sampled snare doubling a live snare; verses often loop-only; the ballads are piano-and-voice until the chorus,\" so the snare is layered, the hat base is 16th and the kick density is low enough that a verse can run on the loop alone. Harmony is called out as a genre departure and is authored as one: \"I–V–vi–IV and IV–I–V–vi — pop rotations rather than country ones,\" which is why no I–IV–V family appears at all here. The melody is \"high tenor with a wide range and pop melisma, syncopated 16th phrasing, and a falsetto post-chorus,\" giving the widest male register and the highest octave-jump probability in this batch against a suppressed straight-eighths share. ⚠ The synth sub is described as sitting under a live bass, i.e. reinforcement, so no `drums.bass808` block is authored and the model keeps the genre's inherited live bass; the pop-style anticipation the entry names lives in `bassline.anticipationProb` instead. ⚠ Confidence is medium, matching the research; the per-track readings are [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the clap offsets, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
